<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trakai Voivodeship</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1413">1413</a>–<a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trakai">Trakai</a> Voivodeship was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-horodlo">Union of Horodło</a> in 1413 from the former Duchy of Trakai. It stretched from the area west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> to the south-western borderlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, with Trakai as its seat. It existed until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition of Poland</a> in 1795, when its lands were divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trakai-voivodeship.jpg" alt="Trakai Voivodeship" width="320" height="347" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M.K. · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trakai_Voivodeship_within_Lithuania_in_the_17th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The voivodeship, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Palatinatus Trocensis and in Polish as Województwo trockie, was divided into four powiats or districts: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Grodno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a>, Upytė and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trakai">Trakai</a>, the last ruled directly by the voivode. Its largest cities were Kaunas, Grodno and Trakai. The voivodes lived in the town of Trakai, near Lake Galvė and north of the Trakai Peninsula Castle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas the Great</a> created the Trakai Voivodeship together with the Vilnius Voivodeship in 1413, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-horodlo">Union of Horodło</a>. He copied the Polish system of administrative division in order to centralise and strengthen his government. The new province replaced the Duchy of Trakai, which had been ruled directly by the grand duke or a close relative such as a brother or son, and the Duke of Trakai gave way to appointed officials: a voivode and his deputy, the castellan.</p><p>The Voivode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trakai">Trakai</a> held one of the most important offices of the Grand Duchy. Chosen from prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> families, the voivodes rivalled only the Voivode of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> and the grand chancellors in power and prestige, and they sat ex officio in the Lithuanian Council of Lords. Holders of the office included Jonas Goštautas, Radvila Astikas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanty-ostrogski">Konstanty Ostrogski</a>, Mikołaj Krzysztof Radziwiłł, known as the Orphan, and members of the Pac, Ogiński and Tyszkiewicz families.</p><p>In 1513 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a> split off the western part of the voivodeship, which was organised as the Podlaskie Voivodeship. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> the province, with the whole Grand Duchy, belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> until the partitions. When the Commonwealth was partitioned in 1795, most of its territory passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, while the lands west of the Neman River became part of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trakai Voivodeship” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1413 the Union of Horodlo turned the duchy into the <b>Trakai Voivodeship</b> of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-trakai">Duchy of Trakai</a></li></ul></section>
